{"reply":"Your earlier note says that your precise, decisive work self and playful old-friends self both felt like you, and that you still wanted to keep the hosting promise as your mood shifted.

In “Of personal identity,” Hume describes the mind as changing perceptions and treats continuity as a succession of related perceptions, rather than an unchanging, simple self ([Hume](https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6)). In Chapter 30 of *The Adventures of Pinocchio*, Pinocchio is drawn to the promised pleasures of the Land of Toys, but says, “I have promised my kind Fairy to become a good boy, and I want to keep my word.”

They offer different lenses: Hume for thinking about change without demanding one fixed self; Pinocchio for noticing the pull between immediate fun and a commitment. Neither has to make one of your sides fake. What would help you take a next step on preparing without feeling that you have to disown the part of you that wants to do something fun?","evidence_uses":[{"source_kind":"memory","evidence_id":"mem_e0d5811c7131b460fc4dd0355645aaa04ffda47d3323a3c7be396a99931c9686","supported_claims":["Your earlier note says that your precise, decisive work self and playful old-friends self both felt like you, and that you still wanted to keep the hosting promise as your mood shifted."]},{"source_kind":"web","evidence_id":"https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6","supported_claims":["In “Of personal identity,” Hume describes the mind as changing perceptions and treats continuity as a succession of related perceptions, rather than an unchanging, simple self ([Hume](https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6))."]},{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg500-v6bdc1734-ch30-ln4114-4172","source_location":"Chapter 30 — Pinocchio, instead of becoming a boy, runs away to the Land of Toys with his friend, Lamp-Wick., source lines 4114-4172","exact_quote":"I have promised my kind Fairy to become a good boy, and I want to keep my word.","supported_claims":["In Chapter 30 of *The Adventures of Pinocchio*, Pinocchio is drawn to the promised pleasures of the Land of Toys, but says, “I have promised my kind Fairy to become a good boy, and I want to keep my word.”"]}],"memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}

