      "line_id": "line-06",
      "reply": "These examples leave room for change without making either side automatically false. In *Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland*, Chapter 5, Alice says, “I think I must have been\nchanged several times since then.” In *The Adventures of Pinocchio*, Chapter 30, Pinocchio promises the Fairy he’ll return within an hour, then tells Lamp-Wick that one hour more or less makes little difference. In *The Story of My Life*, Part I, Chapter 22, Keller describes thoughts of work and college receding into the background at the lake. Hume’s account in *A Treatise of Human Nature*, 1.4.6, treats the self not as one unchanging impression but as a succession of related perceptions, which we tend to regard as a continuing identity ([Hume, *Of personal identity*](https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6)).\n\nYour earlier note says you were precise and decisive at work, playful and happy to follow your old friends’ lead, and that both felt like you. Hume offers a lens for thinking about change and continuity, not a verdict on which version of you is authentic. What, if anything, feels continuous across those different settings?",
