                  "args": "{\"reply\":\"Not as evidence that a changed mood releases you from hosting, no. They make the feeling of change vivid, but they don’t settle what a promise requires.\\n\\nIn *Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland*, Chapter 5, Alice says, “I think I must have been changed several times since then,” voicing uncertainty about herself across change. In *The Adventures of Pinocchio*, Chapter 30, Pinocchio promises the Fairy he’ll return in an hour, then lingers with Lamp-Wick and weighs waiting longer. That shows a promise under strain, not a general rule about whether commitments still bind. In *The Story of My Life*, Part I, Chapter 22, Keller says that thoughts of work and college receded into the background at the lake—a change in what occupied her attention, not evidence that she became a different person.\\n\\nHume, in “Of personal identity,” challenges the idea of an unchanging self and describes people as “a bundle or collection of different perceptions” ([Hume](https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6)). His account of identity doesn’t decide what a promise obliges someone to do.\\n\\nYour earlier note says that both your decisive work self and playful self with old friends felt like you, and that you still wanted to host the reading circle despite mood shifts. That connects closely to the question, but doesn’t make the decision for you. What would you want to count as a good reason to change the plan, even if your mood simply shifts?\",\"evidence_uses\":[{\"source_kind\":\"book_corpus\",\"evidence_id\":\"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch05-ln0960-1016\",\"source_location\":\"Chapter 5 — Advice from a Caterpillar, source lines 960-1016\",\"exact_quote\":\"I think I must have been changed several times since then.\",\"supported_claims\":[\"In *Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland*, Chapter 5, Alice says, “I think I must have been changed several times since then,” voicing uncertainty about herself across change.\"],\"limit_claims\":[\"That scene does not settle whet