      "reply": "Your earlier note says that in a programme meeting you were precise and decisive, and over dinner with your old friends you were playful and happy to follow their lead; both felt like you, and you still wanted to keep the hosting promise when your mood shifted.\nIn “Of personal identity,” Hume describes the mind as changing perceptions and treats continuity as a succession of related perceptions, rather than an unchanging, simple self ([Hume](https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6)). In Chapter 30 of *The Adventures of Pinocchio*, Pinocchio is drawn to the promised pleasures of the Land of Toys, but says, “I have promised my kind Fairy to become a good\nboy, and I want to keep my word.”\n\nThey offer different lenses: Hume for thinking about change without demanding one fixed self; Pinocchio for noticing the pull between immediate fun and a commitment. Neither has to make one of your sides fake. What would help you take a next step on preparing without feeling that you have to disown the part of you that wants to do something fun?",
