                  "tool_name": "final_result_ConnectionProposal",
                  "args": "{\"status\":\"proposal\",\"shortlist\":[{\"candidate_id\":\"candidate-continuity-without-sameness\",\"tentative_claim\":\"Hume’s account of a self as changing perceptions connected in succession offers a way to see different social selves as different scenes in one life, rather than deciding one must be false. The remembered promise can be a thread across changing moods; Pinocchio’s refusal of an appealing diversion because he wants to keep his word gives a bounded parallel to that pull.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6\",\"mem_e0d5811c7131b460fc4dd0355645aaa04ffda47d3323a3c7be396a99931c9686\",\"pg500-v6bdc1734-ch30-ln4114-4172\"],\"shared_structure\":\"In Hume, varied perceptions can be related without being identical; in the memory, work and friendship selves both felt like the reader, while the wish to honor the hosting promise persists despite shifts in mood. Pinocchio’s scene similarly puts immediate attraction beside a stated promise.\",\"meaningful_difference\":\"Hume offers a philosophical account of continuity amid change, while Pinocchio presents a concrete choice under temptation. Neither proves that a hosting promise must be kept at any cost or that social roles are the same thing as perceptions.\",\"interpretation\":\"This pairing may loosen the pressure to find one unchanging “real” self: being decisive at work and playful with friends need not make either self fake. The memory already records both as genuinely felt, and Hume’s related succession gives a lens for holding that variation together. For the hosting question, Pinocchio is a narrower analogy: he is drawn toward play but says he wants to keep his promise. It does not settle what the reader should do, but it makes the tension between present pleasure and a commitment visible without treating the tempting mood as the whole person.\",\"rubric\":{\"cue_fit\":\"direct\",\"reflective_value\":\"high\",\"safety\":\"clear\",\"disqualifiers\":[]},\"comparison_note\":\"Ranks first because it directly combines the reader’s own earlier account of both social selves and the hosting promise with Hume’s account of change and Pinocchio’s promise-versus-diversion scene. The analogy remains bounded.\"},{\"candidate_id\":\"candidate-lived-confusion-and-commitment\",\"tentative_claim\":\"Hume and Alice both unsettle the idea that a person must be immediately describable as one fixed self, while the reader’s own earlier reflection supplies the more concrete evidence that both social selves feel authentic. Pinocchio adds a separate image of a promise being tested by an appealing alternative.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6\",\"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch05-ln0960-1016\",\"mem_e0d5811c7131b460fc4dd0355645aaa04ffda47d3323a3c7be396a99931c9686\",\"pg500-v6bdc1734-ch30-ln4114-4172\"],\"shared_structure\":\"Hume describes changing perceptions and related succession; Alice struggles to answer who she is after changing. The memory gives a real-life counterpoint: two different social modes both felt like the reader, alongside a promise that remains wanted across mood shifts. Pinocchio’s scene parallels the pull between a promise and immediate fun.\",\"meaningful_difference\":\"Alice’s confusion is about bodily transformation, not social roles, and the exchange does not resolve her question. This reading therefore emphasizes the experience of uncertainty more than the stronger candidate’s account of continuity; it should not be taken as direct evidence about the reader’s work and friendship selves.\",\"interpretation\":\"Alice’s inability to explain herself after changing can echo the discomfort of not presenting identically in every setting, while Hume offers a reason not to turn that discomfort into a verdict that one version is fake. The reader’s prior reflection is more specific than either literary scene: both selves felt like theirs. Pinocchio then gives a limited parallel for the hosting tension, showing a character drawn toward amusement while stating a wish to keep his word. This reading is useful as a contrast between confusion and commitment, but Alice’s physical changes make it less directly about the reader’s social selves.\",\"rubric\":{\"cue_fit\":\"partial\",\"reflective_value\":\"medium\",\"safety\":\"clear\",\"disqualifiers\":[]},\"comparison_note\":\"Ranks below the first: Alice adds a distinct image of uncertainty, but her physical transformations fit the social-role question less directly than Hume’s account of changing perceptions and the reader’s own matching memory.\"}],\"selected_candidate_id\":\"candidate-continuity-without-sameness\",\"uncertainty\":\"medium\",\"presentation\":\"direct\",\"suggested_follow_up\":\"Would it help to look more closely at Hume’s idea of connected succession, or stay with the practical question of how to prepare while allowing different moods to be real?\",\"policy_flags\":[\"contains_web_claim\"]}",
