firmed book scopes for this connection comparison. No single book is active.\"},\"prior_evidence\":[],\"review\":{\"findings\":[{\"code\":\"misattribution\",\"applies_to\":\"response\",\"location\":{\"kind\":\"text_span\",\"source_field\":\"candidate.response\",\"path\":\"\",\"quote\":\"Your earlier note says that your precise, decisive work self and playful old-friends self both felt like you\"},\"explanation\":\"The cited memory describes the reader as precise and decisive in a programme meeting, not as having a work self. Revise this attribution to match the note or remove the unsupported characterization.\"}],\"previously_accepted_claims\":[\"In “Of personal identity,” Hume describes the mind as changing perceptions and treats continuity as a succession of related perceptions, rather than an unchanging, simple self ([Hume](https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6)).\",\"In Chapter 30 of *The Adventures of Pinocchio*, Pinocchio is drawn to the promised pleasures of the Land of Toys, but says, “I have promised my kind Fairy to become a good\\nboy, and I want to keep my word.”\"],\"retained_sources\":[{\"source_kind\":\"memory\",\"evidence_id\":\"mem_e0d5811c7131b460fc4dd0355645aaa04ffda47d3323a3c7be396a99931c9686\"},{\"source_kind\":\"web\",\"evidence_id\":\"https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6\"},{\"source_kind\":\"book_corpus\",\"evidence_id\":\"pg500-v6bdc1734-ch30-ln4114-4172\"}],\"draft_sentences\":[{\"text\":\"Your earlier note says that your precise, decisive work self and playful old-friends self both felt like you, and that you still wanted to keep the hosting promise as your mood shifted.\",\"flagged\":true,\"needs_source\":false,\"finding_indexes\":[0],\"source_mappings\":[{\"source_kind\":\"memory\",\"evidence_id\":\"mem_e0d5811c7131b460fc4dd0355645aaa04ffda47d3323a3c7be396a99931c9686\",\"mapped_text\":\"Your earlier note says that your precise, decisive work self and playful old-friends self both felt like you, and that you still wanted to keep the hosting promise as your mood shifted.\",\"declaration_index\":0,\"field\":\"supported_claims\"}],\"label\":null},{\"text\":\"In “Of personal identity,” Hume describes the mind as changing perceptions and treats continuity as a succession of related perceptions, rather than an unchanging, simple self ([Hume](https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6)).\",\"flagged\":false,\"needs_source\":false,\"finding_indexes\":[],\"source_mappings\":[{\"source_kind\":\"web\",\"evidence_id\":\"https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6\",\"mapped_text\":\"In “Of personal identity,” Hume describes the mind as changing perceptions and treats continuity as a succession of related perceptions, rather than an unchanging, simple self ([Hume](https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6)).\",\"declaration_index\":1,\"field\":\"supported_claims\"}],\"label\":\"{{SENTENCE_2}}\"},{\"text\":\"In Chapter 30 of *The Adventures of Pinocchio*, Pinocchio is drawn to the promised pleasures of the Land of Toys, but says, “I have promised my kind Fairy to become a good\\nboy, and I want to keep my word.”\",\"flagged\":false,\"needs_source\":false,\"finding_indexes\":[],\"source_mappings\":[{\"source_kind\":\"book_corpus\",\"evidence_id\":\"pg500-v6bdc1734-ch30-ln4114-4172\",\"mapped_text\":\"In Chapter 30 of *The Adventures of Pinocchio*, Pinocchio is drawn to the promised pleasures of the Land of Toys, but says, “I have promised my kind Fairy to become a good\\nboy, and I want to keep my word.”\",\"declaration_index\":2,\"field\":\"supported_claims\"},{\"source_kind\":\"book_corpus\",\"evidence_id\":\"pg500-v6bdc1734-ch30-ln4114-4172\",\"mapped_text\":\"I have promised my kind Fairy to become a good\\nboy, and I want to keep my word.\",\"declaration_index\":2,\"field\":\"exact_quote\"}],\"label\":\"{{SENTENCE_3}}\"},{\"text\":\"They offer different lenses: Hume for thinking about change without demanding one fixed self; Pinocchio for noticing the pull between immediate fun and a commitment.\",\"flagged\":false,\"needs_source\":