      "reply": "I don’t see Hume’s account backing the “off the hook” conclusion. In §6 of “Of personal identity,” Hume describes changing perceptions and how we attribute identity across related experiences ([Hume, *A Treatise of Human Nature*, 1.4.6](https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6)). His passage addresses personal identity; it doesn’t say that a shift in mood releases someone from a promise.\n\nIn *Pinocchio*, Chapter 30, Pinocchio says, “I have promised my kind Fairy to become a good\nboy, and I want to keep my word.” His friend is urging him toward the Land of Toys, and the scene contrasts that attraction with Pinocchio’s stated intention. It’s a fictional counter-image, not proof of a rule for your hosting plan.\n\nIn an earlier note, you said both your work-self and friend-self felt like you, and that you still wanted to keep the hosting promise when your mood shifted. What would distinguish, for you, a genuine reason to revisit the plan from a momentary pull toward something more fun?",
