                  "args": "{\"reply\":\"I don’t see Hume’s account backing the “off the hook” conclusion. In §6 of “Of personal identity,” Hume describes changing perceptions and how we attribute identity across related experiences ([Hume, *A Treatise of Human Nature*, 1.4.6](https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6)). That passage is about personal identity; it doesn’t say that a shift in mood releases someone from a promise.\\n\\nA different kind of source, *Pinocchio*, Chapter 30, gives a contrasting scene: “I have promised my kind Fairy to become a good boy, and I want to keep my word.” Pinocchio says this while his friend is urging him toward the Land of Toys. It’s a story, not proof of a rule for your situation, but it depicts temptation and a stated wish to keep one’s word coexisting.\\n\\nAnd in an earlier note, you said both your work-self and friend-self felt like you, and that you still wanted to keep the hosting promise when your mood shifted. That doesn’t settle what you should do, but it doesn’t sound like you yourself had treated the promise as belonging to a different person. What would distinguish, for you, a genuine reason to revisit the plan from a momentary pull toward something more fun?\",\"evidence_uses\":[{\"source_kind\":\"web\",\"evidence_id\":\"https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6\",\"supported_claims\":[\"In §6 of “Of personal identity,” Hume describes changing perceptions and how we attribute identity across related experiences ([Hume, *A Treatise of Human Nature*, 1.4.6](https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6)).\"],\"limit_claims\":[\"That passage is about personal identity; it doesn’t say that a shift in mood releases someone from a promise.\"],\"exact_quote\":null},{\"source_kind\":\"book_corpus\",\"evidence_id\":\"pg500-v6bdc1734-ch30-ln4114-4172\",\"source_location\":\"Chapter 30 — Pinocchio, instead of becoming a boy, runs away to the Land of Toys with his friend, Lamp-Wick., source lines 4114-4172\",\"supported_claims\":[\"A different kind