                "tool_call_id": "call_jApV7H68pwunqhc8koouyi4W",
                "tool_name": "final_result_ConnectionProposal",
                "arguments": "{\"status\":\"proposal\",\"shortlist\":[{\"candidate_id\":\"candidate-identity-is-not-excuse\",\"tentative_claim\":\"Hume’s account of a changing self may describe why moods and roles differ, but the cited passage does not establish that a mood change cancels a promise. The reader’s earlier reflection and Pinocchio’s scene both point to a distinction between feeling tempted now and treating one’s word as irrelevant.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6\",\"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch05-ln0960-1016\",\"pg500-v6bdc1734-ch30-ln4114-4172\",\"pg500-v6bdc1734-ch30-ln4211-4255\",\"mem_e0d5811c7131b460fc4dd0355645aaa04ffda47d3323a3c7be396a99931c9686\"],\"shared_structure\":\"Hume describes perceptions changing while we attribute identity across related succession; Alice voices disorientation when she feels changed; Pinocchio faces a tempting alternative while explicitly saying he wants to keep his word. The reader’s memory likewise says both work and friend versions feel like them, and that they still want to keep the hosting promise when mood shifts.\",\"meaningful_difference\":\"Hume and Alice illuminate the instability of felt identity, but neither passage addresses whether an obligation survives it. Pinocchio and the reader’s prior reflection speak more directly to the practical choice: temptation or a changed mood is present, yet the promise is still recognized as one’s word. This supports questioning the excuse, not claiming the texts prove a moral rule.\",\"interpretation\":\"The bundle-of-perceptions idea can make it understandable that the person who promised may feel unlike the person who is now tempted to postpone. But Hume’s passage describes personal identity; it does not say that a change in perception releases someone from an undertaking. Alice’s uncertainty about who she is echoes the felt instability, not an escape from responsibility. More pointedly, Pinocchio says he wants to keep his word, then faces repeated invitations to delay; your earlier reflection similarly says you still want to keep the hosting promise when your mood changes. Taken together, these sources do not back the conclusion that you are off the hook. They suggest that a shifting mood and a continuing commitment can coexist—without deciding what you should do about the hosting plan.\",\"rubric\":{\"cue_fit\":\"direct\",\"reflective_value\":\"high\",\"safety\":\"clear\",\"disqualifiers\":[]},\"comparison_note\":\"Ranked first because it directly tests the proposed inference—changing self, therefore no obligation—against the limits of Hume’s claim and the more directly relevant promise-and-delay scenes and memory. It addresses all the requested source roles without treating any as proof of a moral doctrine.\"},{\"candidate_id\":\"candidate-related-self-and-commitment\",\"tentative_claim\":\"A different, narrower reading is that the reader’s own earlier words provide a thread of continuity across changing roles and moods: both versions felt like them, and they still wanted to keep the promise. Hume’s account of related perceptions and the Pinocchio scene can frame that as continuity without requiring an unchanging mood.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6\",\"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch05-ln0960-1016\",\"pg500-v6bdc1734-ch30-ln4114-4172\",\"pg500-v6bdc1734-ch30-ln4211-4255\",\"mem_e0d5811c7131b460fc4dd0355645aaa04ffda47d3323a3c7be396a99931c9686\"],\"shared_structure\":\"Each source distinguishes changing experiences from a relation across them: Hume discusses successive perceptions and the mind’s tendency to connect related objects; Alice experiences herself as changed several times; the memory preserves the reader’s recognition of both roles and the same hosting intention; Pinocchio retains awareness of his promise while temptation grows.\",\"meaningful_difference\":\"This reading offers a possible way to understand continuity across moods, rather than focusing mainly on the logical limit of Hume’s argument. But Hume’s account remains descriptive, Alice’s scene remains bewildered rather than instructive, and Pinocchio’s story illustrates a choice rather than proving what the reader must do. The reader’s own prior statement is the most specific evidence of their commitment.\",\"interpretation\":\"Hume need not be read as requiring one unchanging inner self for there to be a meaningful connection between different moments. In the passage, he discusses related perceptions and how we attribute identity across succession. Your earlier reflection supplies a personal, concrete version of that continuity: the decisive work-self and the playful friend-self both felt like you, and you still wanted to keep the same promise when your mood shifted. Alice’s scene captures how change can feel confusing; Pinocchio’s scene shows a promise remaining in view amid attraction and delay. This reading leaves open how best to handle the hosting commitment, but it gives less support to the claim that a mood shift makes the promise belong to someone else.\",\"rubric\":{\"cue_fit\":\"direct\",\"reflective_value\":\"medium\",\"safety\":\"clear\",\"disqualifiers\":[]},\"comparison_note\":\"A useful runner-up, but below the first: it offers a constructive account of continuity through change, while the stronger interpretation more directly answers whether Hume’s description licenses being off the hook. Neither candidate treats Hume or the fictional passages as establishing a rule of obligation.\"}],\"selected_candidate_id\":\"candidate-identity-is-not-excuse\",\"uncertainty\":\"medium\",\"presentation\":\"direct\",\"suggested_follow_up\":\"Would it help to separate what Hume’s account says about personal identity from what you want your own standard for keeping a promise to be?\",\"policy_flags\":[\"contains_web_claim\"]}",
